  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "A $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,\u2016x\u2016=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=5,$ ${\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{2},$ and ${\\sigma }_{3}=0,$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse.",
  "worked_solution": "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,\u2016x\u2016=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto the ellipse ${Ax\\,|\\,\u2016x\u2016=1}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2016Ax{\u2016}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $\u2016x\u2016=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is ${\\sigma }_{1}=5,$ the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ must be ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}={5}^{2}.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{max}_{\u2016x\u2016=1}{\u2016Ax{\u2016}^{2}}={5}^{2}\\,\u27f9\\,{max}_{\u2016x\u2016=1}{\u2016Ax\u2016}=5.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is ${\\sigma }_{1}=5.$",
